<article class="env-lemma" id="060B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/060B">Lemma <span data-tag="060B">76.15.9</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ Z \ar[r]_ h \ar[d]_ f &  X \ar[d]^ g \\  W \ar[r]^{h'} &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> be a fibre product diagram of algebraic spaces over $S$ with $h'$ formally unramified. Then $h$ is formally unramified and if $W \subset W'$ is the universal first order thickening of $W$ over $Y$, then $Z = X \times _ Y W \subset X \times _ Y W'$ is the universal first order thickening of $Z$ over $X$. In particular the canonical map $f^*\mathcal{C}_{W/Y} \to \mathcal{C}_{Z/X}$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/060A" data-tag="060A">76.15.8</a> is surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The morphism $h$ is formally unramified by Lemma <a href="/tag/0600" data-tag="0600">76.14.5</a>. It is clear that $X \times _ Y W'$ is a first order thickening. It is straightforward to check that it has the universal property because $W'$ has the universal property (by mapping properties of fibre products). See Lemma <a href="/tag/04CQ" data-tag="04CQ">76.5.5</a> for why this implies that the map of conormal sheaves is surjective. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>